      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse given in the problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the key parameters of the ellipse from the given information.\n2. Use the formula for the distance between the foci of an ellipse.\n\nThe given ellipse is in the standard form of an ellipse centered at the origin with a vertical major axis and a horizontal minor axis. Therefore, we can write the equation of the ellipse in standard form as:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\nwhere \\(a\\) is the semi-major axis length and \\(b\\) is the semi-minor axis length.\n\nFrom the diagram, we can see that:\n- The semi-major axis length \\(a\\) is 3 units (the distance from the center to the topmost point of the ellipse), so \\(a = 3\\).\n- The semi-minor axis length \\(b\\) is 2 units (the distance from the center to the rightmost point of the ellipse), so \\(b = 2\\).\n\nFor an ellipse, the distance between the foci is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c\\) is the focal distance, calculated using the formula:\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{3^2 - 2^2} = \\sqrt{9 - 4} = \\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{5}}\n\\]",
